From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: the guest's Caps Lock follows the Mac's after it lost its lock (#784)

QEMU sent a Caps Lock press whenever the Mac's Caps Lock flag differed from
its own count of the presses it had sent (QKBD_MOD_CAPSLOCK). The guest can
lose its lock without a press from QEMU: a new desktop session, a restart
inside the VM (QEMU keeps running), a press while the guest was still
starting. QEMU's count stayed "on", so nothing was sent, and the next Caps
Lock off on the Mac turned the guest's on: capitals with the Mac's light
off (issue #784, reported by @epicoun), until Caps Lock was pressed outside
the VM.

Now the guest's lock follows what its keyboard's Caps Lock LED reports
(virtio-input's EV_LED through QEMU's LED notifier; Hyprland keeps the LED
with its lock): each LED change is the answer to a press of ours (one per
press, within 500 ms) or the guest's own. A lock lost with the Mac's on is
put back at the next input event; a lock set in the guest (another
keyboard) stays until the Mac's flag changes; a guest whose Caps Lock key
does not lock (Compose, Escape) never answers and gets one press per change
of the Mac's flag, as before. As the guest's keyboard starts, a LED that
disagrees with our presses wins too (a press that came while the guest was
not listening went nowhere); a reset keyboard reports no LED lit
(omacvm-virtio-input-leds-reset.patch). The rules: ui/omacvm-capslock.h.
The notifier is added at the first input event, with the BQL held as for the
events.

Upstream: upstreamable (upstream's ui/cocoa.m has the same count).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/keys/test-caps-lock.sh (the rules, and with the
patched ui/cocoa.m the wiring).
Applies after omacvm-cocoa-modifier-state.patch.

--- a/ui/cocoa.m
+++ b/ui/cocoa.m
@@ -2335,8 +2335,27 @@
     qkbd_state_key_event(kbd, KEY_LEFTSHIFT, left);
     qkbd_state_key_event(kbd, KEY_RIGHTSHIFT, right);
     return true;
+}
+
+/*
+ * OmacVM (#784): the guest's Caps Lock, from what its Caps Lock LED reports
+ * (ui/omacvm-capslock.h, no AppKit in there), or that its keyboard started or
+ * stopped. The LED notifier runs with the BQL held, as handleEventLocked does.
+ */
+#include "ui/omacvm-capslock.h"
+static OmacVMCaps omacvm_caps;
+static bool omacvm_caps_listening;
+
+static void omacvm_caps_led_changed(Notifier *notifier, void *data)
+{
+    omacvm_caps_led(&omacvm_caps, qemu_input_get_leds_mask(dcl.con) & QEMU_CAPS_LOCK_LED,
+                    qemu_clock_get_ms(QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME));
 }
 
+static Notifier omacvm_caps_led_notifier = {
+    .notify = omacvm_caps_led_changed
+};
+
 - (bool) handleEventLocked:(NSEvent *)event
 {
     /* Return true if we handled the event, false if it should be given to OSX */
@@ -2376,12 +2395,21 @@
     }
 
     /*
-     * Caps Lock: a change of its flag is a press and a release. The other
-     * modifiers follow the flags (omacvmSyncModifiers), on every input event:
-     * a change while QEMU was not in front is put right with the next one.
+     * Caps Lock: a press and a release when the Mac's flag and the guest's
+     * lock differ, the guest's lock as its Caps Lock LED reported it (#784: a
+     * new session or a restart inside the VM clears it without a press of
+     * ours). The other modifiers follow the flags (omacvmSyncModifiers), on
+     * every input event: a change while QEMU was not in front is put right
+     * with the next one.
      */
-    if (!!(modifiers & NSEventModifierFlagCapsLock) !=
-        qkbd_state_modifier_get(kbd, QKBD_MOD_CAPSLOCK)) {
+    if (!omacvm_caps_listening) {
+        omacvm_caps_led(&omacvm_caps, qemu_input_get_leds_mask(dcl.con) & QEMU_CAPS_LOCK_LED,
+                        qemu_clock_get_ms(QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME));
+        qemu_input_led_notifier_add(&omacvm_caps_led_notifier);
+        omacvm_caps_listening = true;
+    }
+    if (omacvm_caps_step(&omacvm_caps, !!(modifiers & NSEventModifierFlagCapsLock),
+                         qemu_clock_get_ms(QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME))) {
         qkbd_state_key_event(kbd, KEY_CAPSLOCK, true);
         qkbd_state_key_event(kbd, KEY_CAPSLOCK, false);
     }
